discussion_comments.comment_body:
Confirmed and fixed in 1c240de0 — good catch, and the same class of leak already fixed in `agents.rs` this round. I fixed it there and did not sweep this function for the identical pattern.

`writeln!`, `sync_all` and the source-line composition each returned via `?` with the file already created and no `StagedCommand` for any caller to `discard`. Under `ENOSPC` — precisely when a stray partial file is least welcome — that left a partial payload in `staged-commands`.

The file is now opened in `stage_in`, and everything after it moved into `fill_payload`, whose result is matched once:

```rust
match fill_payload(&mut file, &remove, command, keyword, &path_arg, shell_name) {
    Ok(text) => Ok(StagedCommand { text, payload: Some(path) }),
    Err(err) => {
        let _ = std::fs::remove_file(&path);
        Err(err)
    }
}
```

The open deliberately stays outside that match. `create_new(true)` means a failure there found a file we did not create, and removing it would be wrong.

Test added: `a_path_too_long_to_source_refuses_and_stages_nothing` builds a directory long enough that the rendered line exceeds `MAX_TYPED_COMMAND`, then asserts both the refusal and an empty directory. Characterized — dropping the cleanup arm fails it and names the leaked payload.

That test also revises what I said on the other thread about a near-limit case being too fragile to test. That was wrong: it is only awkward via `XDG_STATE_HOME` near `PATH_MAX`. Through `stage_in`'s explicit directory it needs about 500 characters, which is nowhere near any limit and is entirely stable.

